import{_ as t,o as d,c as a,X as o}from"./chunks/framework.CNGPadWr.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Data contracts","titleTemplate":":title · CellPack Rust","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"rust/api/contracts.md","filePath":"contracts.md"}'),r={name:"rust/api/contracts.md"};function s(n,e,c,i,l,u){return d(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="data-contracts" tabindex="-1">Data contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Data contracts&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Use this reference before connecting external arrays, sampling callbacks, or shared output buffers. Shapes, units, id spaces, and ownership are part of the API contract; a successful compilation alone does not establish them.</p><h2 id="which-layer-accepts-what" tabindex="-1">Which layer accepts what? <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-layer-accepts-what" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which layer accepts what?&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Allocation and ownership</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>scans</code></td><td>Borrowed matrix/record bytes, metadata and caller workspace</td><td>Encoded bytes, decoded arrays, or borrowed <code>Parts</code></td><td>Caller owns outputs and scratch; borrowed views retain dependencies</td></tr><tr><td><code>store</code> writer</td><td>CSR/dense row batches or packed chunks</td><td>A finalized <code>.cpk</code> stream</td><td>Writer owns staging and encoding buffers</td></tr><tr><td><code>store</code> prepared reader</td><td>A file region, ordered row selection, optional column map</td><td>Caller-owned typed dense/CSR arrays</td><td>Selection owns prepared metadata; workspace owns scratch</td></tr><tr><td><code>loader</code></td><td>Immutable registered files, schemas, deterministic accesses</td><td>Leased native output bytes per schema and batch</td><td>Producer owns ring writes; consumer lease delays slot reuse</td></tr><tr><td><code>opt</code></td><td>Loader graph, accesses, frame budget and costs</td><td>Residency plan or online compiler state</td><td>Plan binds to input, schema and sequence identity</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="matrix-inputs" tabindex="-1">Matrix inputs <a class="header-anchor" href="#matrix-inputs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Matrix inputs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Let <code>R</code> be rows, <code>C</code> columns, <code>E</code> stored entries, and <code>W</code> bytes per stored value.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Required representation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>DenseRef</code></td><td>Row-major <code>data</code>; a whole number of rows of <code>C × W</code> bytes</td></tr><tr><td><code>CsrRef</code></td><td><code>R + 1</code> <code>u64</code> row offsets; index and value arrays covering every row interval</td></tr><tr><td><code>CsrView</code></td><td>The same row model with borrowed integer offsets through <code>Offsets</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CellRef</code></td><td>One sparse row, strictly ascending unique column ids; <code>indices.len() × W</code> value bytes</td></tr><tr><td><code>RowRef</code></td><td>One sparse or dense row, interpreted with its declared dimensions and value width</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Values are little-endian bytes, with <code>W ∈ {1, 2, 4, 8}</code>. Chunk encoders require <code>1 &lt;= C &lt;= i32::MAX</code>. Within every CSR row, indices must be nonnegative, strictly increasing, distinct, and below <code>C</code>. Row offsets must describe readable index/value intervals and must not decrease. A complete standalone CSR normally starts at offset zero; views can describe intervals into larger backing arrays.</p><p><code>Indices</code> accepts <code>u16</code>, <code>u32</code>, <code>i32</code>, <code>i64</code>, or <code>u64</code> storage. Signed negatives and ids outside the supported range are invalid. <code>Offsets</code> accepts <code>u32</code>, <code>u64</code>, <code>i32</code>, or <code>i64</code>; signed offsets must be nonnegative. Wider host storage does not expand the codec&#39;s column-id space.</p><p>Dense and CSR source layouts do not force each encoded record&#39;s layout. With automatic layout selection, individual records can be sparse or dense. Explicit sparse zeros are stored entries. In loader semantics, an omitted value of a present column is zero, rather than a missing dimension.</p><h2 id="numeric-types-and-conversion" tabindex="-1">Numeric types and conversion <a class="header-anchor" href="#numeric-types-and-conversion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Numeric types and conversion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>File dtypes are <code>U8/U16/U32/U64/I8/I16/I32/I64/F32/F64</code>. Codec compression preserves stored value bit patterns. It does not normalize, quantize, replace NaNs, or attach biological meanings to values.</p><p>Typed storage reads and <code>scans::CastInto</code> use Rust <code>as</code> semantics for their supported numeric element types. The loader admits a narrower set of pairs:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Stored → output</th><th>Loader support</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Same dtype</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Unsigned → wider unsigned or wider signed</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Signed → wider signed</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Signed → same-width or wider unsigned</td><td>Yes; negative values are not checked before casting</td></tr><tr><td>Any integer → <code>f32</code> or <code>f64</code></td><td>Yes; large integers can lose precision</td></tr><tr><td><code>f32</code> → <code>f64</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Other narrowing, float → integer, <code>f64</code> → <code>f32</code></td><td>Rejected by loader construction</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Use <code>loader::allowed(source, output)</code> for the actual pair check. The low-level cast API and loader admission rules are distinct. Native loader output is typed host representation; file/codec byte streams are little-endian representation.</p><h2 id="shapes-and-capacity" tabindex="-1">Shapes and capacity <a class="header-anchor" href="#shapes-and-capacity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shapes and capacity&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Output extent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Storage dense read</td><td>Exactly <code>ReadSpec.rows × ReadSpec.columns</code> elements</td></tr><tr><td>Storage CSR read</td><td>Exactly <code>rows + 1</code> offsets; index/value capacity at least <code>ReadSpec.nnz</code></td></tr><tr><td>Codec chunk CSR decode</td><td><code>selected_rows + 1</code> offsets and sufficient entry/value capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Codec dense decode</td><td>Byte count from <code>scans::dense_len</code></td></tr><tr><td>Dense loader schema</td><td><code>out_ids.len() × dtype.width()</code> bytes per cell</td></tr><tr><td>Process schema</td><td>Exactly declared <code>cell_bytes</code> per cell</td></tr><tr><td>Leased schema region</td><td><code>batch.rows() × cell_bytes(schema)</code> bytes</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>ReadSpec.nnz</code> is source-based capacity. Column filtering can reduce the returned actual nnz. Ignore unused index/value capacity. A tail batch can have fewer than <code>slot_cells</code> positions; use <code>rows()</code>, not the configured slot size.</p><p>Encoding and decoding sizing APIs distinguish byte capacity from <code>u64</code> workspace words. Do not multiply or divide units implicitly.</p><h2 id="column-and-row-identities" tabindex="-1">Column and row identities <a class="header-anchor" href="#column-and-row-identities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Column and row identities&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Important limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Source column</td><td>Position in one input matrix</td><td>Codec dimension range</td></tr><tr><td>Coded column</td><td>Chunk-local compression order</td><td>Requires the matching chunk permutation</td></tr><tr><td>Standard column</td><td>Application-owned vocabulary id</td><td>Loader builder&#39;s <code>vocab</code></td></tr><tr><td>Output column</td><td>Position in one schema&#39;s panel</td><td><code>out_ids.len()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CellId</code></td><td>Global source row, assigned in registration order</td><td><code>u32</code>; constructors check representable counts</td></tr><tr><td>Position</td><td>Logical output row in the sequence</td><td>Can repeat the same cell</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td>One schema delivery at a position</td><td><code>k / S</code> is position; <code>k % S</code> is schema</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Storage <code>RowSelection::List</code> uses <code>u64</code> file rows. Codec <code>Rows::List</code> uses <code>u32</code> chunk-local rows. Loader <code>Accesses</code> produces global <code>CellId</code> values. These selectors are not interchangeable.</p><p>A storage gene map is <code>source → output</code>. A data-source map is <code>source → standard</code>. A schema lists standard ids in output order. Maps must cover their required input domain, and kept targets must be distinct. <code>UNMAPPED = u32::MAX</code> means dropped, not a valid output or standard id.</p><h2 id="missing-dimensions" tabindex="-1">Missing dimensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#missing-dimensions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Missing dimensions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A source matrix either has a standard column or does not. A present column whose sparse record omits a value produces zero. A dimension absent from that matrix produces the schema default. Defaults never replace present zeros. CSR process rows cannot distinguish these two omitted cases; dense process rows can distinguish them when the default is a sentinel no valid value takes.</p><h2 id="file-and-page-identity" tabindex="-1">File and page identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#file-and-page-identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;File and page identity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A stream is valid after <code>CpkWriter::finish</code> succeeds. A writer may truncate an existing path. Its success is not an atomic publication or fsync guarantee. Keep files immutable while prepared selections, sources, loaders, and plans use them. Loader construction/preparation verifies recorded file identity.</p><p>Packed page sizes are powers of two in <code>[512, 65536]</code> bytes. All matrices of one data source use the builder&#39;s page size. Embedded stream offsets and lengths are supplied by the application. <code>O_DIRECT</code> additionally requires filesystem and physical-offset alignment support.</p><h2 id="borrowing-and-concurrency" tabindex="-1">Borrowing and concurrency <a class="header-anchor" href="#borrowing-and-concurrency" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Borrowing and concurrency&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Lifetime rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Borrowed encoder input</td><td>Keep immutable through the call or the entire <code>PreparedEncoder</code> borrow</td></tr><tr><td><code>Meta</code></td><td>Keep its backing metadata bytes alive</td></tr><tr><td>Decode/encode tables</td><td>Use only with their matching metadata</td></tr><tr><td><code>Parts</code></td><td>Borrows workspace, record, and possibly metadata; consume before reusing them</td></tr><tr><td><code>ReadWorkspace</code></td><td>Exclusive to one read at a time; can be reused afterward</td></tr><tr><td>Loader run</td><td>Borrows loader and sequence; one run holds a loader at a time</td></tr><tr><td>Batch region</td><td>Valid only while its lease is held and backing memory remains alive</td></tr><tr><td>Native callback</td><td>Code/context must outlive every call, be thread-safe, and retain no input pointers</td></tr></tbody></table><p>External ring constructors are unsafe: backing storage must be stable, correctly aligned, sufficiently large, and governed by the slot protocol. An asynchronous device copy is still a reader; release its source lease only after completion.</p><h2 id="failure-and-validation-boundary" tabindex="-1">Failure and validation boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#failure-and-validation-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Failure and validation boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Entry points check declared shapes, capacities, option ranges, and metadata compatibility where documented. Inner record bytes are trusted. Malformed trusted data can cause undefined behavior in optimized paths; the decoder is not an untrusted-data validator.</p><p>A read/decode/process failure can leave partial output. Discard that operation&#39;s output. Loader failure cancels the run and wakes participants. An aborted ring cannot start another run. Report the original non-<code>Aborted</code> error when available.</p><p>See <a href="/cellpack-docs/rust/api/reference-errors.html">errors</a> for exact variants and recovery scope.</p>',35)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
